<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Likely because not everyone's aware that it's now a historical curiosity?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think most people are aware that the UK uses an SI based metric system now and don't imagine that you can go and compare your furlong chain to Her Majesty's anymore. I think the problem is that people for some reason think the system that is in common use in the USA is called &quot;Imperial&quot; and not &quot;US Customary&quot; and are unaware of the differences between these systems. For whatever reason this is a common misnomer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I think it's the general go-to people use when they're referring to &quot;not-SI/metric/that correct thing scientists use&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, but I still don't understand why it is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's also present in the guides</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It says we use &quot;metric and not Imperial&quot;. It further says that we use SI. It doesn't mention the US Customary system at all. I don't think that constitutes permission to use US Customary instead of SI. When someone posts an SCP with feet and you say &quot;don't use Imperial&quot;, and they say &quot;It's okay those are US feet&quot;, is it going to work? No, probably not.</p>
<p>Sure, we don't use Imperial units. We don't use stadia either. More relevantly we don't use US Customary which is what most of these units are actually in (assuming that most of them are being posted by 21st century Americans and not subjects of her Majesty Queen Victoria; which I am guessing is a reasonable assumption).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>one would need to talk to the staff who have science degrees to get that changed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, who is that?</p>
