<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Yes some sources include it for historical reasons. However unlike every other derived unit it isn't formed from arithmetical operations with base units but rather with a conversion factor (again a trivial one; so trivial that the &quot;°C/K&quot; is usually omitted but you actually can't define °C only with base units). You can derive Fahrenheit from K with a conversion factor too. Also unlike <em>every</em> real SI unit you can't use it with the prefixes (that's why &quot;degrees Kelvin&quot; is incorrect).</p>
<p>Edit: Which is to say that some science writing uses degrees Celsius (it depends on the field and the publication), and I think you can probably use it in skips for that reason, but my preference is to just use K, and there's no reason to include both. Which is what I have been trying to say.</p>
</div>
